WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.920
The Yale Peabody
Museum meteorite collection

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.920 align:start size:3% position:97% line:7%
♪

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.674
is the first meteorite
collection in North America.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:11.344
It started with the Weston
Connecticut meteorite

00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:17.142
that was analyzed and described
by Benjamin Silliman in 1807.

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:20.895
And from that one meteorite,
the collection grew

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:24.733 align:start position:0% line:83%
which puts the Yale
meteorite collection

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:29.028 align:start position:0% line:83%
into the top meteorite
collections in the world.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:32.115
[sound of drill]
This crate

00:00:32.323 --> 00:00:35.326 align:start position:0%
contains a roof section

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:38.288 size:84% position:42%
from Park Forest, Illinois,

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:41.875 align:start position:0%
that was hit
by one of the meteorites.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.920
So I don't exactly know how
this fits in here,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.175
but I think it was something
like this

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:52.677 size:78% position:39%
fitting the Tetris model.

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:55.764
This is a slice of the moon
of a meteorite

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.767
that was collected in Libya.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:02.437
Another interesting,
meteorite source that we know

00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:05.440
for sure where it comes from

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:10.445
is a piece of the planet Mars.

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:17.118
These are some of the most
spectacular meteorites.

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:19.704
So it's a slice

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.081
that if you look at it

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:26.503
against light,
it looks like stained glass.

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:28.713
It is a beautiful piece.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:32.300
Meteorites
give us the means to understand

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.179
the mineralogical, chemical,
physical,

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.516
and biological evolution
of the solar system.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.851
The value
of a meteorite collection,

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:44.854
just like the value
of a mineralogy collection

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:49.484
or the value of a paleontology
collection, the scientific value

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.821
lies in the fact that new data
can be taken

00:01:52.821 --> 00:01:57.659
from these objects
as science makes progress.

00:01:57.826 --> 00:02:00.078
It all boils down to curiosity.

00:02:00.078 --> 00:02:01.329
That's the key word.

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:03.873
I am a curious individual.

00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:06.584
I think most people are curious.

00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:10.630
Meteorites are the
only objects that take us

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.341
out of the comfort

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:17.679
of our home planet
out into space.

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.640 align:start size:6% position:94% line:14%
♪♪

